    75	\section{Introduction}
    76	
    77	\subsection{Background and Motivation}
    78	Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) have become indispensable tools in precision agriculture, enabling applications such as crop monitoring, spraying, mapping, and yield estimation \cite{zhang2012,tsouros2019,puri2017,mogili2018,hunt2018}. Global adoption has accelerated dramatically: the agricultural drone market was valued at approximately USD~5.9 billion in 2022 and is projected to exceed USD~20 billion by 2030, driven by the need to optimise labour costs and chemical inputs across increasingly large farm units \cite{tsouros2019,mogili2018}. As agricultural operations scale up, coordinating multiple UAVs simultaneously---rather than deploying a single drone sequentially---offers transformative gains in coverage efficiency: a five-UAV swarm can theoretically cover five times the area in the same flight window, contingent on effective path planning and collision avoidance \cite{chung2018,gupta2016}.
    79	
    80	However, coordinating multiple UAVs (swarms) presents significant optimization challenges, particularly in path planning where objectives include minimizing energy consumption, maximizing coverage, and ensuring collision avoidance \cite{chung2018,gupta2016,bekmezci2013,hayat2016}. These objectives are inherently conflicting: maximizing coverage requires dense flight paths that increase energy consumption, while collision avoidance introduces inter-agent coordination constraints that scale superlinearly with fleet size. Classical deterministic methods such as A* and Dijkstra's algorithm struggle with this combinatorial complexity beyond small fleet sizes, creating an opening for metaheuristic approaches \cite{aitsaadi2022}.
    81	
    82	Swarm Intelligence (SI) algorithms, inspired by collective behaviors in nature (ant colonies, bird flocks, bee swarms), offer promising solutions for these multi-agent optimization problems \cite{dorigo2006,kennedy1995,blum2008,yang2010,beni2005,bonabeau1999,yang2013}. Their key advantages for agricultural UAV path planning are threefold: (1) \textit{scalability}---population-based search scales naturally with fleet size; (2) \textit{distributed decision-making}---agents can execute planning locally without centralised communication; and (3) \textit{robustness}---the stochastic nature of SI algorithms provides resilience against local optima that deterministic planners cannot escape. Despite these advantages and exponential growth in publications (2021--2025), systematic analysis reveals persistent gaps that limit practical deployment:
    83	
    84	\begin{itemize}
    85	  \item \textbf{Metric inconsistency:} Only 42.4\% of studies report quantitative time metrics, 39.4\% report energy consumption, and 21.2\% specify convergence criteria, preventing cross-algorithm comparison.
    86	  \item \textbf{Validation gap:} 78.8\% lack hardware validation in real field conditions, relying exclusively on simulation.
    87	  \item \textbf{Environmental simplification:} 75.8\% do not model wind, weather, or dynamic obstacles---factors critical for agricultural operations.
    88	  \item \textbf{Scalability limitations:} Most studies focus on single-UAV or small fleets ($<$5 UAVs), far below the fleet sizes required for commercial agricultural operations.
    89	\end{itemize}
    90	
    91	These gaps collectively indicate that the field has achieved algorithmic sophistication but has not yet translated it into deployable systems. This review provides the first systematic documentation of these gaps to guide the community toward practical deployment.
    92	
    93	\subsection{Research Questions}
    94	This systematic review addresses the following research questions, following the PRISMA 2020 guidelines \cite{page2021}:
    95	\begin{itemize}
    96	  \item \textbf{RQ1:} What swarm intelligence algorithms are most frequently applied to multi-UAV path planning in agriculture (2021--2025)?
    97	  \item \textbf{RQ2:} What quantitative metrics are reported, and how consistent are they across studies?
    98	  \item \textbf{RQ3:} What research gaps exist, and how can they be categorized systematically?
    99	  \item \textbf{RQ4:} What are the priority directions for future research based on gap criticality?
   100	\end{itemize}
   101	
   102	\subsection{Contributions}
   103	This paper makes four key contributions:
   104	\begin{enumerate}
   105	    \item \textbf{Comprehensive Taxonomy:} Classification of 33 papers across 14 algorithm categories (PSO, ACO, ABC, SSA, GWO, DBO, NOA, DOA, etc.), extending prior taxonomies \cite{chung2018,brambilla2013,puente2022,sharma2022}.
   106	    \item \textbf{Quantitative Synthesis:} Extraction and comparison of metrics including execution time, energy consumption, and convergence criteria, addressing standardization gaps identified in recent surveys \cite{phung2021,pan2022,aitsaadi2022}.
   107	    \item \textbf{Four-Dimensional Gap Framework:} 171 documented gaps organized by Technological (51, 29.8\%), Practical (52, 30.4\%), Methodological (39, 22.8\%), and Theoretical (29, 17.0\%) dimensions. This framework builds upon systematic review methodologies from robotics and software engineering literature \cite{brambilla2013,bayindir2016}.
   108	    \item \textbf{Actionable Research Agenda:} Three prioritized directions for community advancement, emphasizing experimental validation, dynamic environment integration, and the AgriSwarm-Bench open benchmarking framework.
   109	\end{enumerate}
